WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.040
Generally, movies are pretty harmless forms
of escapist entertainment, but every so often

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.280
a film will get its hooks into reality and
completely screw over a living, breathing

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.280
person.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:14.409
A movie can ruin an actor's or filmmaker's
career, or in extreme cases, cause the death

00:00:14.409 --> 00:00:16.279
of people not even involved with the film.

00:00:16.279 --> 00:00:21.140
The following movies touched some very real
lives… and things didn't end well for anyone

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.140
involved.

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.009
Snow White and the Seven Dwarfs

00:00:24.009 --> 00:00:28.380
As it was one of the first animated feature
films ever made, you'd think Snow White and

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.570
the Seven Dwarfs would've turned its leading
lady, Adriana Caselotti, into a major star.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.740
After all, the film was a big hit and a cinematic
landmark, but despite playing in one of the

00:00:37.740 --> 00:00:43.270
most beloved movies ever made, Caselotti largely
vanished from the Tinseltown scene after working

00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:44.270
on the Disney film.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:49.710
According to some theories, Walt Disney refused
to let Caselotti use her voice in any other

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:54.010
program, keeping her under a tight contract
so that no one would know the secret voice

00:00:54.010 --> 00:00:55.040
behind the princess.

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:05.240
However, Disney didn't start contracting actors
until 1946, so that theory is unlikely.

00:01:05.240 --> 00:01:09.020
Instead, it seems that Caselotti's own talent
betrayed her.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.950
Disney himself said that he couldn't cast
her again because her voice was just too easy

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.530
to identify, while still refusing to let her
work elsewhere.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:21.150
Add that to the fact that she was never credited
on the original film until it was too late,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.720
and Caselotti was left with a bad contract,
and a slumbering career.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:25.720
Peeping Tom

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.220
Once upon a time, Michael Powell directed
some of the greatest films of English cinema,

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:33.220
including Black Narcissus and The Red Shoes.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.880
But everything changed in 1960, when Powell
directed Peeping Tom, a wild departure from

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.140
his previous work.

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:44.020
Peeping Tom focuses on a shy, repressed young
photographer who gets his kicks by murdering

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:46.970
women with a blade attached to his camera
tripod.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.720
While tame by today's standards, audiences
at the time were absolutely shocked.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:56.450
Peeping Tom deals with all kinds of uncomfortable
topics, from child abuse to pornography.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:01.280
British critics lost their minds, labeling
the film as "perverted" and "beastly."

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:05.220
Powell was exiled from the British film industry
and could only make movies in other countries,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.770
none of which lived up to his old classics.

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:13.500
As a result, the man and his movie faded into
obscurity until 1979, when Martin Scorsese

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:14.830
re-released the film.

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:18.871
These days, Peeping Tom is considered one
of Powell's best films and the British Film

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:24.160
Institute labeled it the 78th greatest British
film ever—but there's no denying this 1960

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.269
flick completely killed Powell's career.

00:02:27.269 --> 00:02:28.269
Le Mans

00:02:28.269 --> 00:02:31.299
Steve McQueen wanted to make the greatest
racing movie ever.

00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:35.909
Unfortunately, things didn't pan out the way
McQueen had hoped when he was filming Le Mans.

00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:40.159
The film's original director, John Sturges
had his own vision for the film, and eventually

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:42.450
left the project over creative differences.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:46.599
By the end, the movie had gone so far over
budget that McQueen was forced to give up

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:51.330
his salary and the making of Le Mans would
ultimately cause his company, Solar Productions,

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:52.390
to collapse.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:56.200
But at the end of the day, at least McQueen
made it out of Le Mans in one piece.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.420
The same can't be said for David Piper.

00:02:58.420 --> 00:03:03.400
A Formula One racer working on the film, Piper
was driving a Porsche 917 for a scene and

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:06.500
lost control of the car, severely injuring
his leg.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:10.100
The wound became infected, and the doctors
were forced to partially amputate.

00:03:17.569 --> 00:03:22.079
The injury effectively ended his racing career,
and it was all for a movie that's largely

00:03:22.079 --> 00:03:24.400
been forgotten by most moviegoers.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.400
Last Tango in Paris

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:30.829
After the success of Last Tango In Paris,
actress Maria Schneider claimed she couldn't

00:03:30.829 --> 00:03:35.290
deal with her newfound fame, and soon, she
was using cocaine and heroin as "an escape

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.620
from reality."

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:40.310
Schneider eventually hit such a rough patch
that she tried to commit suicide.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.760
But it's become apparent that Schneider was
probably suffering from more than unwanted

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:44.799
media attention.

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:50.621
The movie's infamous scene was in the script,
and the on-screen assault was simulated, but

00:03:50.621 --> 00:03:53.310
the use of butter wasn't in the screenplay.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:58.209
As the director, Bernardo Bertolucci explained
in a 2013 interview, this was an impromptu

00:03:58.209 --> 00:04:03.310
decision that he and Brando made on-set, and
they deliberately didn't tell Schneider what

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:04.480
they were going to do.

00:04:09.189 --> 00:04:13.180
After Bertolucci's confession, The Guardian
posited that Schneider's behavior after Last

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:16.910
Tango had more to do with PTSD than her sudden
fame.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:17.970
The Message

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:23.590
In 1977, a director Moustapha Akkad released
The Message, a desert epic in the vein of

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:24.970
Lawrence of Arabia.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:29.610
Only instead of focusing on an English adventurer,
the film tells the story of the Prophet Muhammad

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:30.930
and the rise of Islam.

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:35.380
True, you're not supposed to actually depict
Muhammad, but Akkad got around this by having

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:39.420
actors speak directly at the audience, as
if the camera itself was the prophet.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.810
But despite Akkad's attempts at making a peaceful,
inoffensive film, quite a few people took

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:45.590
his message the wrong way.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:50.110
Several countries banned the film, but even
worse, someone decided to stage a protest…

00:04:50.110 --> 00:04:51.640
with swords and shotguns.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:56.630
Hamaas Abdul Khaalis and a group of 11 took
over multiple buildings in Washington D.C.,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:58.730
and claimed 149 hostages.

00:04:58.730 --> 00:05:01.930
Tragically, this attack wasn't without casualties.

00:05:01.930 --> 00:05:06.240
When the group stormed the District Building,
Khaalis and his group shot and murdered security

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:09.480
guard Mack Cantrell and a reporter named Maurice
Williams.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:13.970
Fortunately, the rest of the hostages were
freed 39 hours later, and Khaalis was sentenced

00:05:13.970 --> 00:05:17.060
to life in prison, where he died in 2003.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:18.250
The Goonies

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:22.210
It's safe to say that the people who love
The Goonies really, really love it.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:26.160
In fact, fans from as far away as Europe have
made the pilgrimage to Astoria, Oregon, to

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.280
see The Goonies house for themselves.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:29.280
You know the one.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.930
It's two stories tall, has cool stuff in the
attic, and you have to do the truffle shuffle

00:05:32.930 --> 00:05:36.830
to get inside.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:41.080
But while the Astoria house has brought joy
to a lot of fans, not everyone is so happy

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.430
with the home's popularity.

00:05:42.430 --> 00:05:47.210
Sandi Preston bought the house in 2001, and
at first, she was totally cool with fans showing

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:48.210
up to take photos.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:53.200
However, that all changed in 2015, when she
wrapped her house with blue tarps and set

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.440
out signs saying her home was now off-limits
to the public.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.420
So what prompted such a radical change?

00:05:58.420 --> 00:06:02.350
In recent years, the amount of tourists coming
to see the house was on the rise.

00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:07.310
Shockingly, during the summer of 2015, up
to 1,500 people dropped by her home every

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:08.360
single day.

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:12.200
While some were on their best behavior, many
of these fans didn't care all that much about

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:12.720
manners.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:24.420
Eventually, it was just all too much for Preston
who decided it was time to take her house

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:25.420
back.

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:26.420
Thanks for watching!

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.330
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:30.160
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

